From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU interpreter: cheaper hot path (same results, same cycles)

What broke:
  Nothing is wrong, but slow: in Ben 10: Protector of Earth's first level
  about half of the game thread's time was in the VU1 interpreter
  (44 of 60 gdb stack samples had VU1Interpreter frames, findings/19
  s.8). A callgrind profile of 40 VU1 workloads captured in the level and
  replayed offline (the port's tools/vu1_replay, findings/20) put the
  time in the per-pair and per-cycle bookkeeping more than in the
  arithmetic.

Why:
  - commitReadyPipelines walked every pipeline array (flags, FDIV, EFU,
    stores, VF/VI/ACC writes: about 50 slots) on every simulated cycle,
    even when no entry was due.
  - flushPipelines stepped one cycle at a time until PATH1 had copied the
    whole XGKICK packet (2 cycles per qword), paying those scans for each
    cycle; progressXgkick copied each qword byte by byte with a modulo.
  - getDecodedInstructionPairForPc returned a copy of a large struct for
    every pair.
  - calculatePairReadyCycle and markPairWrites looped over all 15 VI
    registers and all 4 lanes; normalizeOperand, called for every FMAC
    operand, was an out-of-line call from the upper-instruction file.

The fix (same results, same cycle counts):
  - m_nextCommitCycle keeps the earliest readyCycle of the queued entries
    (updated at every insertion, found again by each commit pass, reset
    with the scheduler); commitReadyPipelines returns at once below it.
  - One bit mask per pipeline array marks the valid slots; the commit pass
    visits only those, in slot order, so entries commit in the same order
    and on the same cycle as with a full scan.
  - flushPipelines: when only XGKICK is left, it steps the cycle counter
    and PATH1 directly (all advanceOneCycle does then); progressXgkick
    copies a qword with one memcpy when it does not wrap.
  - getDecodedInstructionPairForPc returns a reference (into the cache, or
    to one scratch pair for uncached code).
  - After a code upload (new generation) the decoded-pair cache only
    forgets its pairs (one valid byte per pair) and decodes each pair when
    it is first used, instead of decoding all 2048 at once: in the first
    level the game uploads VU1 code several times per frame (217 VU1 and
    63 VU0 rebuilds in 15 s), and each run touches a part of the code.
    Decoding a pair only reads that pair's 8 bytes.
  - The hazard check, markPairWrites and run() visit only set VI bits and
    the lanes used; normalizeOperand is inline.
  Tested: the 40 captured workloads give bit-identical results (VF, VI,
  ACC, Q, P, I, R, PC, MAC, clip, status, cycle count, VU data memory, GS
  memory) before and after; instructions executed for them fall from
  296.5 M to 185.1 M (callgrind). All 451 ps2xTest cases pass (VU1 timing
  and XGKICK included). In the game: findings/19 s.9 and findings/20.

